('P', '**Related evidence.** Across 49 studies, positive religious coping went with better adjustment to stress, and negative religious coping (feeling punished by God, doubting God’s care) with worse (Ano & Vasconcelles, 2005). Across 97 outcome studies, therapy adapted to clients’ religious beliefs worked about as well as standard therapy for distress and produced added spiritual benefits (Captari et al., 2018).'),
('P', '**Honest summary for teaching:**'),
('N', 'Cognitive, dynamic, and schema therapies for Cluster C disorders are the evidence base. Faith integration supports them; it does not replace them.'),
